Endpoint:- for every request a route is regitered thats called an endpoint.

Route:- HTTP method + URL, ex:- GET /api/health
meaning: When someone sends a GET request to /api/health, execute this code.

Axios:- is a JavaScript library used to make HTTP requests to a backend API. Since React does not provide a special API client for talking to a backend. Automatic JSON handling, Request interceptors, Response interceptors, Easier error handling, Request cancellation, Automatic timeout configuration. 
React builds the interface. Axios is the messenger that carries data between the React frontend and the Express backend.

MongoDB:- database is a large container, collection is a folder which saves files, document is a file.

Schema:- What fields exist?, What type are they? , What rules apply? (blueprint)

Model:- Model lets us interact with MongoDB. (Construction company using the blueprint)


Note
1. The browser, a form, or React creates a request containing both a method and a path. Express receives both, looks for a route with the same method and path, executes its callback, and sends a response back.

Why 

1. mongoose:-
MongoDB speaks MongoDB. Node.js speaks JavaScript.Mongoose is translator between them.

2. dotenv:- it loads content of .env into process.env

3. react-router-domLets :- lrts you have multiple pages like /login, /dashboard

4. axios:- Makes API calls to your backend (cleaner than fetch)

Separation of concerns:-
1. server.js exists:- 
Responsibility: Start the application.

2. app.js exists:-
Responsibility: Configure Express application.


3. db.js:- Connects database

4. frontend/src/main.jsx:- This is the entry point of your entire React app. It finds the <div id="root"> in your index.html and mounts your app inside it.

5. frontend/src/App.jsx:- This file is your map of pages. It says: when the URL is /, show Login. When it's /register, show Register. And so on. We'll add more routes later as we build more pages.

6. frontend/.env:- This tells your React app where your backend lives. Later when we deploy, we'll change this to the live backend URL.

7. frontend/src/api/axios.js:- Think of this like saving a contact in your phone. Instead of dialing the full number every time, you just call "Backend" and it knows where to go. Every API call we make from now on will use this API object instead of typing http://localhost:5000/api every single time.

8. frontend/src/pages/Register.jsx:- useState holds the value of each input field as the user types

9. frontend/src/pages/Login.jsx:- useNavigate — after login we redirect the user to /dashboard automatically, localStorage — we save the token in the browser so the user stays logged in even after refreshing the page

10. frontend/src/pages/Dashboard.jsx:- a. When the page loads, useEffect checks localStorage for a saved user
b. If no user is found, it redirects to login — this is a basic protected route
c. If user is found, it displays their name, streak, completed days and garden stage
d. The logout button clears localStorage and sends them back to login

11. frontend/src/api/affirmations.js:- This is just a plain JavaScript object. No database needed. Each key is a category name, and each value is an array of affirmations for that category.

12. frontend/src/pages/Reflection.jsx:- a. Step 1 shows a dropdown of categories
b. Step 2 only appears after a category is chosen
c. The affirmation list changes based on which category was picked
d. Custom category and custom affirmation both show a text input when selected
e. On submit we send everything to POST /api/reflections with the JWT token in the header




authRoutes.js: Defining API endpoints (POST /register)
authController.js: Registration logic, Login logic, Validation logic
User.js: User schema, User model, Database interaction


server.js
    │
    ▼
imports app.js
    │
    ▼
starts Express application



Request and Response:- information comting and going back from server

jwt.sign(...) :- generates a digitally signed token.

